/-!
# Continuous conormal sequence for a formal power series quotient

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tags 00RU and 02HQ, with the continuous
inverse-limit argument in the DAG
(blueprint: lem-trf-formal-power-series-continuous-conormal).

Tensor factors are interchanged by the canonical tensor symmetry so that the quotient
ring acts on the left factor. Positive truncation exponent `r` is indexed by `r - 1`
in `KaehlerDifferential.Continuous`.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace MvPowerSeries

/-- The conormal sequence for continuous differentials of a finitely presented quotient
of a formal power series algebra is right exact. Here `J` is the variable ideal,
`I` is generated by the given list `f`, and the quotient variable ideal is `J.map`.
The formulas specify both arrows on the generators used in the source.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 00RU (`lemma-differential-seq`) and Tag 02HQ
(`lemma-differential-mod-power-ideal`), continuous version
(blueprint: lem-trf-formal-power-series-continuous-conormal).

Proof:
1. By `lem-trf-formal-power-series-continuous-free`, the ambient continuous module M
   is free on the differentials of the variables. Thus T = R tensor M is finite,
   generated by these differentials tensored with 1. Let L be generated by the
   differentials of the f_j and let N = T/L. Differentiation followed by this
   quotient is an F-derivation from D to N.
2. A field is Noetherian. By `lem-weil-pbc-power-series-noetherian` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-power-series-variables-complete`, D is Noetherian and J-adically
   complete. Apply `lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-complete-over-complete-noetherian`
   to the finite D-module N. Its canonical map to lim N/J^r N is bijective.
   Since the action factors through R, J^r N equals (JR)^r N.
3. Write h in I as a finite sum of a_j f_j. Leibniz shows that its differential
   in N vanishes: df_j is killed by L and f_j acts by zero. Thus the derivation
   descends to delta : R to N, sending each variable to its generator in N.
4. Project M to the differentials of D/J^r, then use D/J^r to R/(JR)^r.
   Extend by R-scalars to T. The relation differentials vanish, so this factors
   through phi_r : N to Omega_(R/(JR)^r)/F. The maps commute with transitions
   on universal differentials, giving Phi : N to the continuous module C.
5. Leibniz gives delta((JR)^(r+1)) contained in (JR)^r N: differentiating a
   product of r+1 ideal elements leaves at least r factors in every coefficient.
   Hence universality gives eta_r : Omega_(R/(JR)^(r+1))/F to N/(JR)^r N.
6. The eta_r commute with quotient transitions, as checked on differential
   generators. For c in C, the shifted family eta_r(c_(r+1)) is compatible.
   Completeness of N reconstructs a unique R-linear Psi : C to N.
7. On each variable generator eta_r phi_(r+1) is reduction modulo (JR)^r N.
   Those generators span N, so separatedness gives Psi Phi = id.
8. Since (JR)^r annihilates the rth differential module, phi_r factors through
   N/(JR)^r N. The composite with eta_r is the differential transition, by its
   value on every universal differential. Coordinatewise Phi Psi = id_C.
9. The composite T to N to C is therefore surjective with kernel L, which is
   the image of the first displayed arrow. The coordinate calculation in step 4
   gives the asserted value on every pure tensor of a universal differential.
   The same quotient construction covers n = 0, s = 0 and redundant relations;
   no regular-sequence assumption or division by an integer is used. -/
theorem formal_power_series_continuous_conormal
    (F : Type u) [Field F] (n s : ℕ)
    (J I : Ideal (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F))
    (hJ : J = Ideal.span (Set.range (X : Fin n → MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F)))
    (f : Fin s → MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F)
    (hgen : Ideal.span (Set.range f) = I) (hI : I ≤ J) :
    ∃ (a : (Fin s → MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I) →ₗ[MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I]
        (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I) ⊗[MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F]
          KaehlerDifferential.Continuous F (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F) J)
      (b : (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I) ⊗[MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F]
          KaehlerDifferential.Continuous F (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F) J
        →ₗ[MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I]
          KaehlerDifferential.Continuous F (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I)
            (J.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk I))),
      (∀ j : Fin s, a (Pi.single j 1) =
        (1 : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I) ⊗ₜ[MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F]
          KaehlerDifferential.continuousD F (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F) J (f j)) ∧
      (∀ (h : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F) (c : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I),
        b (c ⊗ₜ[MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F]
            KaehlerDifferential.continuousD F (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F) J h) =
          c • KaehlerDifferential.continuousD F (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) F ⧸ I)
            (J.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk I)) (Ideal.Quotient.mk I h)) ∧
      Function.Exact a b ∧ Function.Surjective b := by
  sorry

end MvPowerSeries
